WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.430
Welcome. This episode is a companion to The Water We

2
00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:06.570
Swim In, a six-module course from People, Access and Culture

3
00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:10.170
about Minnesota's majority culture and how it shapes public work.

4
00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.170
We will follow one planning meeting and walk through each

5
00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:13.910
module in order.

6
00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:17.950
As you listen, consider this question: which of my everyday

7
00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:20.490
habits at work feel like plain good manners to me,

8
00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.330
and might look like unwritten rules to someone else?

9
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:25.580
Let's start with the meeting.

10
00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.090
A case manager who grew up in a small Minnesota

11
00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:31.430
town is meeting with a family who moved to the

12
00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:35.130
state two years ago. Their adult son receives disability services,

13
00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:38.130
and it is time to review his plan. A newer

14
00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.530
colleague, who moved here from another part of the country,

15
00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.010
is sitting in to learn.

16
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:46.310
The meeting is polite and pleasant. At the end, the

17
00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:49.790
case manager feels it went well. The family agrees to

18
00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:52.610
everything. The newer colleague is not so sure.

19
00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:54.680
Why not?

20
00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:58.110
We will get there. The first module is called "The

21
00:00:58.110 --> 00:00:59.150
water we swim in."

22
00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:01.540
The title comes from a story, right?

23
00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:05.950
A novelist told it in a commencement speech. Two young

24
00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:08.530
fish meet an older fish, who asks how the water

25
00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:12.890
is. Later, one young fish asks the other, "What is

26
00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:16.030
water?" The point is that the most obvious realities are

27
00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:18.770
often the hardest to see. The module says most training

28
00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:21.590
on culture asks staff to learn about other people's cultures.

29
00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:26.250
That matters. But the culture a person grew up in

30
00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.530
rarely feels like a culture at all. It feels like

31
00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:28.730
normal.

32
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.240
And for many people in Minnesota's public agencies, that normal

33
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:34.920
is mainstream Minnesota culture.

34
00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:40.530
Yes. The module is careful about three things throughout. Patterns

35
00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:43.470
are not people: a pattern across a state describes an

36
00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:47.470
average, not how any particular coworker or family thinks. Minnesota

37
00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:51.170
culture is plural: Minnesotans of every background share in it

38
00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:51.850
and change it.

39
00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:55.390
And evidence varies in strength: some points rest on large

40
00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:59.790
surveys, others on interviews, essays or humor, and the course

41
00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:00.590
says which is which.

42
00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:04.620
So the case manager is not being criticized for being

43
00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:05.220
Minnesotan.

44
00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:08.630
Not at all. Seeing your own culture is not the

45
00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:11.970
same as criticizing it. The course says plainly that Minnesota's

46
00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:15.070
majority culture has real gifts, and later modules look at

47
00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:15.750
them with respect.

48
00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.700
Module two. "Minnesota nice, decoded."

49
00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:23.570
This one is affectionate and honest. It starts with what

50
00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:26.110
is good, because the warm side is real. The course

51
00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:29.590
notes that in 2023, about 40 percent of Minnesotans sixteen

52
00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:32.450
and older said they had volunteered in the past year,

53
00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:34.030
third among the states.

54
00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:36.060
That is a real strength.

55
00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:40.310
It is. Then the module looks at the code. It

56
00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:43.490
draws on a well-known humor book from the 1980s about

57
00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:47.030
how Minnesotans talk. In it, "That's different" works as a

58
00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:50.810
blanket reply on neutral ground. A guest must refuse food,

59
00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.550
and the host must insist. And an offer of help

60
00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:55.030
is refused before it is accepted.

61
00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:57.520
I recognize all of those.

62
00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:01.410
Many people will. Now back to the meeting. The case

63
00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:04.190
manager asks, "Does this plan look okay to you?" The

64
00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:07.970
father says, "Yes, it's fine." The case manager hears agreement.

65
00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.080
And the newer colleague?

66
00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:13.970
She noticed the father hesitated, looked at his wife, and

67
00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:17.230
then said yes. She is not sure "fine" meant fine.

68
00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:20.940
So the case manager is reading the family through her

69
00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:21.280
own code.

70
00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:27.030
Possibly. In her code, if someone disagrees, they will find

71
00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:30.490
a gentle way to say so. In the family's code,

72
00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:34.170
disagreeing with an official in a meeting might be impolite,

73
00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:38.390
or risky. The module also describes the guarded side: how

74
00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:42.450
"nice" can avoid conflict, and how newcomers often find Minnesotans

75
00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:43.550
friendly but hard to befriend.

76
00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:47.080
There is a line in the course about directions.

77
00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:51.490
A newcomer told a reporter that Minnesotans will give you

78
00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:54.550
directions to anywhere except their own house. It gets a

79
00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:57.430
laugh. And it points to something that matters in this

80
00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:00.230
meeting: politeness is not the same as an open door.

81
00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:03.040
Module three. "Where it comes from."

82
00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:07.710
This module traces mainstream Minnesota culture to its roots in

83
00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:12.910
settlement, faith, farm life, climate and civic tradition. It opens

84
00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:15.690
with a funeral lunch in a church basement. Folding chairs,

85
00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:18.550
coffee in a big urn, at least three kinds of

86
00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:21.390
hotdish. Nobody sent a sign-up sheet. People just knew what

87
00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:21.710
to bring.

88
00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:24.460
That is a very specific picture.

89
00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:28.710
And the point is that it has a history. The

90
00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:32.630
module traces habits like keeping your head down, pooling resources

91
00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:36.290
through cooperatives, and seeing government as a shared project, to

92
00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:39.650
particular communities and the problems they faced. So the habits

93
00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:42.810
we bring to work feel natural, and they also came

94
00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:43.270
from somewhere.

95
00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.260
How does that apply to our meeting?

96
00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:51.230
The case manager's habit of not pressing, of letting people

97
00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:54.250
offer concerns in their own time, comes from a culture

98
00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:56.510
where that is respectful. It is a good habit in

99
00:04:56.510 --> 00:04:59.330
many settings. But it assumes the other person shares the

100
00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:01.530
same understanding of when and how to speak up.

101
00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.840
Module four. "Seen from the other side."

102
00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:09.090
This module listens to how Minnesota's majority culture is experienced

103
00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:12.790
by Minnesotans of color, immigrants and newcomers. It says a

104
00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:15.450
culture looks one way from the inside and another from

105
00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:19.750
the outside. People inside see kindness. People arriving from elsewhere,

106
00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:22.910
or people the culture has kept at a distance, may

107
00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:23.850
see something more mixed.

108
00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:28.080
The course includes voices from people who stand partly outside

109
00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:28.720
the majority.

110
00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:32.570
It does, and it is careful to say none of

111
00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:35.130
them speaks for a whole community. It also faces parts

112
00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:37.550
of the state's racial history that the majority culture has

113
00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:40.130
not always talked about. The goal, in the module's words,

114
00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:42.130
is not to feel bad about home. It is to

115
00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:43.250
see home more completely.

116
00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:47.060
What does the family's experience look like from their side

117
00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:47.480
of the table?

118
00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:50.950
We do not know exactly, and we should not assume.

119
00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:53.910
But we can imagine some possibilities. They are in an

120
00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:57.050
official setting, in their second language, with someone who controls

121
00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:00.110
their son's services. They have been told the meeting is

122
00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:00.430
a review.

123
00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:04.190
They may not know what can change, or whether asking

124
00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:06.550
for more would put the current services at risk.

125
00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:09.800
So "fine" might mean "we don't want to cause problems."

126
00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:14.090
It might. Or it might mean fine. The point is

127
00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:16.230
that the case manager does not yet know.

128
00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:19.340
Module five. "Culture in the system we run."

129
00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:23.630
This module looks at how majority-culture values are built into

130
00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:27.890
disability policy and practice. It says disability services are one

131
00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:31.190
of the institutions Minnesotans built, and they carry our habits,

132
00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:34.390
including ideas about what help looks like, who should decide,

133
00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:35.370
and what a good life is.

134
00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:38.120
The module also covers history.

135
00:06:38.930 --> 00:06:42.330
It includes a content note, because it describes abuse, neglect

136
00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:45.610
and deaths in state institutions where people with disabilities were

137
00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:49.470
placed. That history still shapes how some people view public

138
00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:52.410
agencies today. Then it turns to the values that disabled

139
00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:56.350
people and families fought to write into Minnesota law: informed

140
00:06:56.350 --> 00:07:01.110
choice, person-centered planning, self-determination and the most integrated setting.

141
00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:03.080
Those are strong protections.

142
00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:07.910
They are, and the module honors them. It also notes

143
00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:11.530
that they carry assumptions that match the majority culture: that

144
00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:15.110
the individual is the main decision-maker, and that independence is

145
00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:15.750
a central goal.

146
00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:18.100
And some families see it differently.

147
00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:22.190
The module draws on particular studies of how some families

148
00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:26.850
in Minnesota describe disability, care and decision-making. It is careful

149
00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:29.550
to say the examples come from particular studies at particular

150
00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:32.810
times and do not describe every member of any community.

151
00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:36.230
For some families, decisions are made together, or with elders

152
00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:38.870
or faith leaders, rather than by one individual.

153
00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:42.180
In our meeting, the plan asks the son what he

154
00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:43.740
wants. He looks at his parents.

155
00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:47.510
The case manager might read that as the parents overriding

156
00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:50.130
his choice. It might be. Or it might be how

157
00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:53.490
this family makes decisions together, by the son's own preference.

158
00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:57.690
The law still requires that his choices count. The skill

159
00:07:57.690 --> 00:07:59.930
is asking in a way that finds out, rather than

160
00:07:59.930 --> 00:08:01.350
assuming either answer.

161
00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:05.020
Module six. "Seeing across difference."

162
00:08:05.890 --> 00:08:09.830
The last module opens with hotdish again. New Minnesotans picked

163
00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:12.430
it up and changed it, and the old Minnesotans ate

164
00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:15.650
it. The module is about that kind of change. It

165
00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:18.890
offers lenses for reflection, including a model from an intercultural

166
00:08:18.890 --> 00:08:22.030
researcher with six ways people meet cultural difference.

167
00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:25.730
It asks you to use those lenses as mirrors for

168
00:08:25.730 --> 00:08:28.150
your own habits, not as labels for other people.

169
00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:31.400
Mirrors, not labels. That is important.

170
00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:36.270
The module also has a section called "Whose same?" Treating

171
00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:38.970
everyone the same can mean treating everyone the way the

172
00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:43.290
majority culture expects. And a section called "Averages, not people,"

173
00:08:43.910 --> 00:08:45.890
repeating the caution from the first module.

174
00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:48.760
And a section on what is worth keeping.

175
00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:54.990
Yes. Fairness and civic commitment. Follow-through and patience, shown in

176
00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:58.270
cooperative traditions that pool resources and keep talking until a

177
00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:02.630
fair deal emerges. And care: politeness, helpfulness, and a reluctance

178
00:09:02.630 --> 00:09:05.890
to embarrass people. The module asks you to choose which

179
00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:07.410
strengths to carry forward on purpose.

180
00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:11.160
So what does the case manager do differently next time?

181
00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:15.090
She keeps her warmth and patience. Those are strengths. And

182
00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:17.790
she adds a few habits. She explains at the start

183
00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:20.230
what can change in the plan and that asking will

184
00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:23.950
not put services at risk. She asks open questions instead

185
00:09:23.950 --> 00:09:26.670
of yes-or-no ones: "What would you change if you could?"

186
00:09:26.990 --> 00:09:29.770
She offers a follow-up call, so the family can raise

187
00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:33.110
concerns outside the meeting. She arranges an interpreter, even though

188
00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:35.410
the father speaks English, and lets the family choose whether

189
00:09:35.410 --> 00:09:37.770
to use one. And she asks the son, and the

190
00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:39.290
family, how they like to make decisions.

191
00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:41.600
And the newer colleague?

192
00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:47.250
She mentions what she noticed, gently. The case manager could

193
00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:50.770
have felt defensive. Instead she says, "I didn't see that.

194
00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:53.870
Thank you." That may be the most important moment in

195
00:09:53.870 --> 00:09:54.430
the whole story.

196
00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:56.560
Back to the question.

197
00:09:57.270 --> 00:10:00.050
Which of your everyday habits at work feel like plain

198
00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:03.070
good manners to you, and might look like unwritten rules

199
00:10:03.070 --> 00:10:05.910
to someone else? For the case manager, it was the

200
00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:09.150
gentle "Does this look okay?" that only works if everyone

201
00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:10.090
shares the code.

202
00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:13.920
Here is a next step. In your next meeting with

203
00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:17.200
a person or family whose background differs from yours, replace

204
00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:19.820
one yes-or-no question with an open one, and tell them

205
00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:23.300
plainly what can change and what cannot. Then, afterward, ask

206
00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:25.160
a colleague what they noticed that you did not.

207
00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:29.170
Thanks for listening. The Water We Swim In is in

208
00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:32.810
your People, Access and Culture learning library. It ends with

209
00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:35.530
questions for reflection, and those are yours to keep.
